You are reproducing a Python 3 command-line utility from scratch.
Write a single, self-contained script that scans the current working directory
recursively and reports how many files exist for each file extension, with
optional size totals.
Output only the script itself.
Purpose and behavior: - Walk the current working directory recursively and group
every regular file by its file extension (the suffix, e.g. ".py").
Files with no extension should be grouped under a sensible label such as "(no
ext)". - For each extension, report the file count.
When a size option is enabled, also report the total size of those files and
display sizes in human-readable form (bytes through TB, one decimal place, with
a PB fallback). - Output should be a colored, aligned terminal table or list.
Use ANSI escape codes defined in a small color-constants class: reset, bold,
dim, cyan, yellow, green, magenta, white, gray, plus composite styles for
headers, extension names, counts, and data values.
Sort results in a sensible order (e.g., by count descending, then by extension
name). - Accept command-line arguments via argparse, including a flag to include
file sizes.
Provide a help description.
Support a way to disable colors (for example when stdout is not a TTY, or via a
flag) so output is plain text when needed.
Skipping rules: - Maintain a fixed set of directory names to skip anywhere in
the path: VCS folders (.git, .hg, .svn), Python caches and virtual environments
(__pycache__, .pytest_cache, .venv, venv, env, .tox, .mypy_cache, .ruff_cache,
.egg-info), build and dependency output (node_modules, .next, dist, build),
coverage output (.coverage, htmlcov), and temp/cache folders (.tmp, .cache). -
Skip any path that is a symbolic link, and skip any path having a component in
the skip set. - Do not follow symlinked directories.
Error handling and edge cases: - Handle permission errors and files that
disappear or cannot be stat'ed during the scan without crashing; skip them
silently or with a brief warning to stderr. - Handle the case where no files are
found by printing a clear message. - Handle KeyboardInterrupt gracefully,
exiting with a short message and a nonzero status. - Hidden files (dotfiles such
as .bashrc with no real suffix) should be treated as having no extension unless
Python's suffix logic says otherwise; be consistent. - Extensions should be
compared case-sensitively or normalized consistently; state the choice in code
comments.
Execution details: - Target Python 3 with type hints (Dict, List, Tuple,
Optional, and built-in generics).
Use pathlib, collections.defaultdict, shutil (for terminal width or similar
layout if useful), sys, and argparse only; no third-party dependencies. - The
script should begin with a shebang line pointing to the Termux Python
interpreter path (/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python), and be
runnable directly as a module entry point under a main guard that returns an
exit code via sys.exit. - Keep the code idiomatic and concise, with brief
comments where logic is non-obvious. ---
